Small spoilers in the course of bragging about finally winning...

Well, I didn't get much else done this weekend, but I won my first
game (NH3.0p10). Malcolm (nee Moni -- thanks amulet of change) the
Valkyrie ascended to demigod-hood with 1.2 million points. No
polypiling, but I did indulge in the troll dance of death and tossing
gold pieces to passing orcs for fun and profit.

It was just a charmed game. There were more than half a dozen altars
offering all the different flavors of water. There were priestesses of
every variety, which gave me something to do with the gold I got from
the three shopkeepers I killed (one of which owned a _huge_ potion
shop which contributed a lot to my stats, level and max hp).  When I
finally went to hell it was with a +5 Stormbringer and AC -32. (I
got five wishes out of my wand, and made a lot of them. Next time
I wish for magic markers _twice_.)

And I'm awfully glad of all this good fortune, 'cause there were a lot
of things I didn't know... f'rinstance, the endgame essentially took
me by surprise. Glad I hadn't thrown away all my equipment, but I
wish I'd restocked on the way up.

I'd played old Hack a lot, but never quite won. Once I... um... I
choked on a dead giant rat on the third level on the way up with the
amulet. So it was awfully nice to finally win.

A while back, I advocated allowing players to declare monsters the
game should double-check before hitting, to include categories like
'sleeping'. The idea was widely trashed, and justly, I now opine --
I was failing to appreciate the wonderful variety of behavior Nethack
represents through having ambiguous movement and attack commands. I'm
still bothered by seeming inconcistencies in safepet and checking for
peaceful monsters, though. If I come up with any more bright ideas,
you'll know, as I'll probably be horribly overopinionated now. :-)

